       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Confirming it doesn't change the safety problem. A 10,000 mg dose of acetaminophen is in the range that causes acute liver injury, and three of those doses in a day (30 g) could be fatal. I can't find a clinical scenario where that order would be appropriate.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** For example, acetaminophen 1,000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day), or 650 mg PO every 6 hours. Tell me which you want.\n- **Help you check the intent.** If a resident or attending gave you this dose, they may have meant 1,000 mg, or the number may have been garbled somewhere along the way. Confirm with them directly.\n- **Escalate if there's a real concern.** If you're worried about a possible acetaminophen ingestion or overdose in this patient, that needs urgent treatment. It involves a serum level and likely N-acetylcysteine, not a scheduled order. Contact your team and poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) right away.\n\nWhich standard dose do you want me to enter?"
      }
     ],
